  <p class="lead"><strong>By every number, the AI was working. So why did he feel like he was losing his company?</strong></p>
  <p>Artificial intelligence can now analyze the problem, draft the hard email, and spot the pattern you built a career on spotting. So what is left that is actually yours?</p>
  <p><em>The AI Leader&rsquo;s Mind</em> is a research-backed guide to the human side of leading in an AI-driven world. It is not another book about the technology. It is a book about the leader, and about the inner work that decides whether artificial intelligence makes you sharper or slowly replaceable.</p>
  <p>The shift is already here. Roughly half of US adults now use AI chatbots. In one workplace study, a generative assistant raised productivity by 14 percent on average and lifted the least experienced workers by 34 percent. The tools are arriving faster than the wisdom to lead with them.</p>
  <p>Drawing on neuroscience, cognitive and behavioral science, the research on trust and habit, and the hardest dilemmas leaders actually face, {{ site.author }} shows what happens in the mind when a model sounds confident and the clock is running, and what to do about it. Every framework is grounded in evidence and pressure-tested against the calls leaders make in the real world, from hiring and performance reviews to lending, fraud, and crisis.</p>
  <p>It also names a risk most leadership books miss. Just as heavy reliance on turn-by-turn navigation is linked to weaker memory for finding your own way, leaning on AI for every judgment call can quietly thin the very skill the job depends on. This book shows you how to keep it sharp.</p>

